---
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import ArticleCard from "../../components/ArticleCard.astro";

const articles = (await getCollection("articles"))
  .filter((article) => article.data.category === "Food Prices")
  .sort((a, b) => b.data.pubDate.valueOf() - a.data.pubDate.valueOf());
---

<BaseLayout title="Food Prices 2026 | Food Supply Crisis Report" description="Track food-price inflation, cereal prices, energy costs, fertilizer, shipping and the supply shocks that reach consumers.">
  <section class="hero"><div class="shell"><p class="eyebrow">Topic Hub</p><h1>Food Prices</h1><p>Why food prices move, where pressure is building and what the latest evidence says.</p></div></section>
  <section class="section"><div class="shell">
    <h2>Global commodity prices are not the same as grocery inflation</h2>
    <p>The FAO Food Price Index tracks monthly international prices for a basket of traded food commodities. A national consumer price index measures what households pay in a particular country. Those measures can move in different directions because local crops, exchange rates, taxes, processing, transport, wages and retail margins affect the price reaching a shopper.</p>
    <p>For a useful comparison, identify the commodity and period first, then compare the international benchmark with the country's own CPI and market-price data. A rise in the global index is a warning signal, not proof that every food category is becoming scarce or more expensive everywhere.</p>
    <p>Primary reference points: <a href="https://www.fao.org/newsroom/detail/fao-food-price-index-edges-down-in-october-amid-subdued-demand/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FAO's October 2026 Food Price Index release</a> and each country's statistical-office CPI release.</p>
  </div></section>
  <section class="section"><div class="shell"><div class="card-grid">
    {articles.map((article) => <ArticleCard article={article} />)}
  </div></div></section>
</BaseLayout>
